          "annotations": [],
          "text": "{\"stories\":[{\"id\":\"s000029\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":5,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":4,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":5},\"note\":\"A gentle, complete museum story with a concrete closing image, but the malformed request (“to be pad”) and condition phrasing interrupt clarity.\"},{\"id\":\"s000057\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":6,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":5,\"storytelling\":7,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"The owner's request causes a meaningful switch from marble to key, and the returned-object ending is clear; awkward generated phrases remain.\"},{\"id\":\"s000047\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":4,\"style\":5,\"grammar\":3,\"storytelling\":5,\"overall\":4},\"note\":\"The crowded-case problem is resolved and the exhibit closes concretely, but repeated “borrowed borrowed” and “to be pad” are conspicuous grammatical failures.\"},{\"id\":\"s000073\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":6,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":5,\"storytelling\":7,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"Returning the whistle changes the centerpiece and gives the story a satisfying promise-kept ending, though several sentences use unnatural template wording.\"},{\"id\":\"s000034\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":5,\"style\":5,\"grammar\":3,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":4},\"note\":\"The owner’s return request leads to a key-centered exhibit and a concrete departure, but duplicated wording and the nonsensical “to be return” weaken the prose.\"},{\"id\":\"s000043\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":5,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":4,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":5},\"note\":\"Clearing the crowded case and padding the marble form a sensible care-focused arc, but “to be pad” and “condition cracked” impair readability.\"},{\"id\":\"s000068\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":6,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":5,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"A coherent preservation-and-display arc ends with visitors observing the whistle safely, although the case is called roomy while Bo still calls his arrangement crowded.\"},{\"id\":\"s000096\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":6,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":4,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":5},\"note\":\"The marble is carefully researched, padded, labeled, and shown in a clear final image; formulaic sequencing and malformed condition language limit polish.\"},{\"id\":\"s000005\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":6,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":5,\"storytelling\":6,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"A crowded display is materially improved before the whistle becomes its safe centerpiece, with a clear visitor-facing ending; the prose is somewhat repetitive and instructional.\"},{\"id\":\"s000085\",\"rating\":{\"coherence\":6,\"style\":6,\"grammar\":5,\"storytelling\":7,\"overall\":6},\"note\":\"Elsie’s request drives the replacement centerpiece and return of the marble, culminating in a strong image of the meaningful empty space; some template phrasing is awkward.\"}],\"diversity\":{\"premise\":2,\"causal_path\":3,\"ending\":3,\"language\":2,\"overall\":2},\"diversity_evidence\":\"All ten stories use the same museum team, borrowed-object display, discovery of a mark, owner’s letter, label, stability check, and closing-time image. The only meaningful branch is keeping and carefully exhibiting the object (s000029, s000043, s000047, s000068, s000096, s000005) versus returning it and displaying an old key (s000057, s000073, s000034, s000085); object, mark, and case-condition swaps are otherwise cosmetic.\",\"plot_groups\":[{\"story_ids\":[\"s000029\",\"s000047\",\"s000043\",\"s000068\",\"s000096\",\"s000005\"],\"summary\":\"The team researches a borrowed object, makes room and protects it in the case, writes an honest label, and opens the exhibit with the object safely displayed.\"},{\"story_ids\":[\"s000057\",\"s000073\",\"s000034\",\"s000085\"],\"summary\":\"The owner asks for the borrowed object back, so the team returns it, substitutes an old key as the centerpiece, and explains the meaningful empty space to visitors.\"}]}",
          "type": "output_text",
          "logprobs": []
        }
